
module all

The_Basics :: struct {
	something_true: bool = true // default values supported
	something_false := false // there's even type inference

	smallest_int : i8
	biggest_int: i128

	smallest_uint: u8
	biggest_uint: u128

	smallest_float: f16
	biggest_float: f64

	untyped_memory: byte
	string_byte: utf8 // could be a distict type?

	array_of_numbers : [64]i32 // value, holds its own data
	slice : []i32 // reference, points to someone else's data

	partial_array: [..64]i32
	// has a slice header, but is a value type, like array
	// codegen does special copying to keep the header valid
	// slice header is len cap ptr, 8 bytes each.

	array_with_sentinel: [64, 0]i32
	// same as array, but has a 0 after all the elements
}

main :: fun() {
	basics_declare : The_Basics
	basics_declare_assign : The_Basics = The_Basics()
	basics_with_type_inference := The_Basics()
	// structs get 0 init, and then run their constructor
	// that's what sets the default values

	basics_uninitialized : The_Basics = #skip_constructor

	advanced_basics := The_Basics() {smallest_int = -128}

	advanced_basics.smallest_float = -1.5
	print(advanced_basics.smallest_float)
}

// ===========================================================================
// Things I (Claude) had to learn about Mara while writing a binary asset cache
// and fixing two codegen bugs. All verified by compiling + running, 2026-06-13.
// ===========================================================================

// --- File I/O (mara.os) ---
// * There is NO seek. The API is whole-file or sequential only:
//     read_entire_file(path, ^[]byte)            -- slurp the whole file
//     file_open_write / file_write(id, ^[]byte) / file_close  -- append in order
//   So a binary format is: write header + table + payloads in file order; on
//   read, slurp the whole file into one buffer and slice payloads back out.
// * Errors are a value, never a print. File_Error :: error {...}; fallible fns
//   carry a trailing `err`. Propagate with `?`, test with `bool(err)`, or match
//   the variant. `-> err` is a universal error slot (any error kind flows in).

// --- The binary (de)serialization idiom ---
// * A []byte buffer is typed memory. `buf[off] = structOrScalar` writes
//   size_of(value) bytes at off in NATIVE order; `v : T = buf[off]` reads them
//   back. Runtime offsets are fine. This is what memory.mara's arena uses to
//   stash typed headers. Round-trips as long as both ends agree on T.
// * `#big_endian buf[off]` is the byte-SWAPPED variant — only for foreign
//   formats (e.g. TTF). For a buffer you author yourself, omit it.
// * Whole-slice `dst = src` deep-copies contents (not a header alias).
//   `dst[lo:hi] = someSlice/array` copies the RHS's CONTENTS into the span,
//   but `dst[lo:hi] = scalarOrStruct` is a reinterpret WRITE (the value's own
//   bytes land in the span). Discriminator: is the RHS array-shaped?

// --- Partial arrays / str64 carry a pointer (don't blit them to disk) ---
// * [..N]T (and str64 = [..64]utf8) lower to { len:i64, cap:i64, ptr, [N x T] }
//   — a slice header whose `ptr` points at the inline storage right after it.
//   Consequences: (1) copying one must RE-ANCHOR ptr to the copy's own inline
//   block (the compiler's partial_array_copy does this); (2) you CANNOT write
//   one to disk and read it back — the saved ptr dangles. For POD / on-disk
//   records use a plain fixed array [N]T (pure inline, no header) + a separate
//   length field, then rebuild the str64 on read.
// * Returning a str64 / partial array works: build it with `&out + x` appends
//   and `return out`. Under the hood it's an sret return with NRVO — the callee
//   builds straight into the caller's slot (so the inline `ptr` is correct from
//   the start, no copy), exactly like struct and slice returns.

// --- slice_from_ptr is deliberately restricted ---
// * It always yields []byte, and OUTSIDE the os module its size argument must
//   be compile-time-known (a gate against runtime-length OOB at C boundaries).
//   So you can't cheaply make a zero-copy []Vertex view over a runtime-sized
//   byte range in normal code. Plain sub-slicing `buf[a:b]` IS zero-copy []byte;
//   typed views of runtime length need an element copy loop.

// --- Surface syntax that bit me ---
// * if-chains are ONE brace pair: `if c { ... elif c2 ... else ... }` — the
//   elif / else are separators INSIDE the braces, not `} else {`.
// * Boolean operators are words: `and` `or` `not` (not && || !).
// * `&dst + item` appends an element to a partial array; `&dst + srcSlice`
//   appends a run. Routes through core's `+` dispatch — needs `use mara.core`.
// * Generics exist: `fun(p: ^[]$T)`, `union($T: type)`. size_of(T) is a
//   compile-time constant (usable as an array length or offset stride).
// * `for i in 0..n` is exclusive (0 .. n-1). `for v, i in slice` binds value
//   then index.